@page "/topbar"

<CatHead Title="Topbar and user" Tier="@CatHead.Tier1">
    The header strip: the status and version chips on the right, the app switcher, and the user
    widget with its menu. The search box in it has a page of its own, <a href="/search">header search</a>.
</CatHead>

<CatGroup Title="Header bar">
    <CatExample TExample="Examples.Topbar.VersionTag" Title="Version tag" Demo="ex-demo--block ex-demo--flush">
        <p>
            <code>.topbar-version</code> — which build the reader is looking at, in the topbar's right
            cluster beside the status indicator. Put a version the app already knows in it: an
            assembly informational version, a CI build number, a short commit sha; an
            <code>&lt;a&gt;</code> to a release page carries the class as well as the
            <code>&lt;span&gt;</code> does.
        </p>
    </CatExample>

    <CatExample TExample="Examples.Topbar.BrandedHeader" Title="Brand colour" Demo="ex-demo--block ex-demo--flush">
        <p>
            <code>--topbar-bg</code>, <code>--topbar-fg</code>, <code>--topbar-muted</code> and
            <code>--topbar-hover</code> recolour the header and what sits straight on it: a
            <code>.topbar-btn</code> and a neutral <code>.btn--ghost</code> that is a child of the bar or
            of a <code>.topbar-section</code>. <code>--topbar-height</code> sets its height and the
            sidebar brand's with it. <code>.topbar--sticky</code> keeps it at the top while the document
            scrolls, for a page without the frame's own scroll container.
        </p>
    </CatExample>

    <CatExample TExample="Examples.Topbar.QuietText" Title="Quiet text" Demo="ex-demo--block ex-demo--flush">
        <p>
            Your own words in a <code>.topbar-section</code> &mdash; a context label, a line of state
            &mdash; take <code>.text-muted</code> or <code>.eyebrow</code> like anywhere else, and they
            paint in <code>--topbar-muted</code> there, so they follow a coloured header. The sidebar and
            the bottom bar do the same with <code>--sidebar-muted</code> and
            <code>--bottombar-muted</code>; a menu or a panel opened from the header is a surface of its
            own and keeps the page's muted. Give a header your own colour, and set
            <code>--topbar-muted</code> beside it at 4.5:1 or more.
        </p>
    </CatExample>
</CatGroup>

<CatGroup Title="Menus">
    <CatExample TExample="Examples.Topbar.AppSwitcher" Title="App switcher" Demo="ex-demo--block ex-demo--flush">
        <p>
            <strong>Live &mdash; press the grid.</strong> A <code>.topbar-btn</code> with
            <code>popovertarget</code> opens a <a href="/popover">popover</a> holding
            <code>.app-switcher</code>: one <code>.app-switcher-item</code> link per app, with its mark in
            <code>.app-switcher-icon</code> and its name under it. Mark the app the reader is in with
            <code>aria-current="page"</code>. The platform opens it, closes it on an outside click and on
            <kbd>Escape</kbd>, and a popover holding a switcher opens towards whichever side of the button
            has room, so it works from either end of the header.
        </p>
    </CatExample>

    <CatExample TExample="Examples.Topbar.UserMenu" Title="User menu" Demo="ex-demo--block ex-demo--flush">
        <p>
            The panel is the library's one dropdown style, <code>.menu</code> — see
            <a href="/menu">Menus</a> for its items, labels and separators. Only the anchoring belongs
            to the frame, and <code>.user-widget</code> is <code>position: relative</code> so nothing
            is measured; opening it, the outside click and <kbd>Escape</kbd> are your app's state,
            <code>.menu-scrim</code> goes <em>immediately before</em> the panel because coming first in
            the markup is what puts the panel above it (the demo omits it), and do not declare
            <code>role="menu"</code> — the items are ordinary links and buttons that tab.
        </p>
        <p>
            A choice the menu switches, such as the warehouse the reader works in, marks the one in use
            with <code>aria-current="true"</code> and the library tints and ticks it. The panel always
            opens under the header at its trailing edge; <code>--panel-placement</code>, which places the
            panels anchored to a trigger, does not move it.
        </p>
    </CatExample>
</CatGroup>
